{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Xob2MJQdbGCHQUcfF3KjUs","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd026-cut\n     2→title: \"cmd/cut: Remove Sections from Lines\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  macOS BSD cut lacks the --complement and --output-delimiter flags present in GNU cut.\n     6→  Scripts that invert field selections or use a different output delimiter depend on GNU\n     7→  behavior. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gcut,\n     8→  installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations, verified by differential\n     9→  testing via pkg/testutils.\n    10→\n    11→goals:\n    12→  - G1: Extract selected byte ranges, character ranges, or delimited fields from each line of input, matching GNU cut behavior.\n    13→  - G2: Support -b (bytes), -c (characters), and -f (fields) selection modes with range lists.\n    14→  - G3: Support -d to configure the field delimiter and --output-delimiter to control the separator in the output.\n    15→  - G4: Support --complement to invert the selection (output what was not selected).\n    16→\n    17→requirements:\n    18→  R1:\n    19→    title: Byte and character selection (-b, -c)\n    20→    items:\n    21→      - R1.1: -b LIST must extract the specified byte positions from each line. LIST is a comma-separated list of ranges; each range is N, N-M, N- (from N to end), or -M (from start to M). Bytes are 1-indexed.\n    22→      - R1.2: -c LIST must extract the specified character positions. Under LC_ALL=C, -c and -b are equivalent (one byte per character). The flag is provided for compatibility.\n    23→      - R1.3: With -b or -c, newline characters are not counted as part of the line; they are passed through to terminate each output line.\n    24→      - R1.4: Lines shorter than the selected range produce no bytes for the out-of-range positions; only the bytes that exist are output.\n    25→\n    26→  R2:\n    27→    title: Field selection (-f, -d, -s)\n    28→    items:\n    29→      - R2.1: -f LIST must extract the specified fields from each line, where fields are delimited by the character given with -d (default tab). LIST uses the same range syntax as -b.\n    30→      - R2.2: -d DELIM must set the input field delimiter to the single character DELIM. The delimiter must be exactly one byte. When the output delimiter is not explicitly set with --output-delimiter, the input delimiter is also used as the output delimiter.\n    31→      - R2.3: -s must suppress lines that do not contain the delimiter. Without -s, lines with no delimiter are printed unchanged.\n    32→      - R2.4: --output-delimiter STRING must use STRING as the separator between selected output fields, replacing the default (which is the input delimiter).\n    33→\n    34→  R3:\n    35→    title: Complement mode (--complement)\n    36→    items:\n    37→      - R3.1: --complement must invert the selection; the output contains the bytes, characters, or fields NOT specified by the range list.\n    38→      - R3.2: --complement is compatible with -b, -c, and -f. It is not compatible with -s.\n    39→      - R3.3: With --complement and -f, fields not in the list are output in their original order, separated by the output delimiter.\n    40→\n    41→  R4:\n    42→    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    43→    items:\n    44→      - R4.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n    45→      - R4.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened, printing the error to stderr. Processing continues for remaining files.\n    46→      - R4.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n    47→      - R4.4: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n    48→\n    49→non_goals:\n    50→  - cmd/cut does not support multi-byte delimiters; -d accepts exactly one byte.\n    51→  - cmd/cut does not implement locale-aware multi-byte character cutting for -c; LC_ALL=C is assumed.\n    52→\n    53→acceptance_criteria:\n    54→  - \"AC1: printf 'a:b:c\\\\n' | cut -d: -f2 produces 'b\\\\n', matching gcut -d: -f2.\"\n    55→  - \"AC2: printf 'abcdef\\\\n' | cut -b2-4 produces 'bcd\\\\n', matching gcut -b2-4.\"\n    56→  - \"AC3: printf 'a:b:c\\\\n' | cut -d: -f1,3 produces 'a:c\\\\n', matching gcut -d: -f1,3.\"\n    57→  - \"AC4: printf 'a:b:c\\\\n' | cut -d: -f2 --complement produces 'a:c\\\\n', matching gcut --complement.\"\n    58→  - \"AC5: printf 'no-delimiter\\\\n' | cut -d: -f2 -s produces empty output, matching gcut -s.\"\n    59→  - \"AC6: Differential test against gcut passes for all required test cases.\"\n    60→  - \"AC7: cmd/cut compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    61→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7f934305-abec-4afb-9510-aae792fc87a6","uuid":"a660415c-934f-4af7-87b1-c08282fce04b","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/354/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd026-cut.yaml","content":"id: prd026-cut\ntitle: \"cmd/cut: Remove Sections from Lines\"\n\nproblem: |\n  macOS BSD cut lacks the --complement and --output-delimiter flags present in GNU cut.\n  Scripts that invert field selections or use a different output delimiter depend on GNU\n  behavior. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary (gcut,\n  installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations, verified by differential\n  testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Extract selected byte ranges, character ranges, or delimited fields from each line of input, matching GNU cut behavior.\n  - G2: Support -b (bytes), -c (characters), and -f (fields) selection modes with range lists.\n  - G3: Support -d to configure the field delimiter and --output-delimiter to control the separator in the output.\n  - G4: Support --complement to invert the selection (output what was not selected).\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Byte and character selection (-b, -c)\n    items:\n      - R1.1: -b LIST must extract the specified byte positions from each line. LIST is a comma-separated list of ranges; each range is N, N-M, N- (from N to end), or -M (from start to M). Bytes are 1-indexed.\n      - R1.2: -c LIST must extract the specified character positions. Under LC_ALL=C, -c and -b are equivalent (one byte per character). The flag is provided for compatibility.\n      - R1.3: With -b or -c, newline characters are not counted as part of the line; they are passed through to terminate each output line.\n      - R1.4: Lines shorter than the selected range produce no bytes for the out-of-range positions; only the bytes that exist are output.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Field selection (-f, -d, -s)\n    items:\n      - R2.1: -f LIST must extract the specified fields from each line, where fields are delimited by the character given with -d (default tab). LIST uses the same range syntax as -b.\n      - R2.2: -d DELIM must set the input field delimiter to the single character DELIM. The delimiter must be exactly one byte. When the output delimiter is not explicitly set with --output-delimiter, the input delimiter is also used as the output delimiter.\n      - R2.3: -s must suppress lines that do not contain the delimiter. Without -s, lines with no delimiter are printed unchanged.\n      - R2.4: --output-delimiter STRING must use STRING as the separator between selected output fields, replacing the default (which is the input delimiter).\n\n  R3:\n    title: Complement mode (--complement)\n    items:\n      - R3.1: --complement must invert the selection; the output contains the bytes, characters, or fields NOT specified by the range list.\n      - R3.2: --complement is compatible with -b, -c, and -f. It is not compatible with -s.\n      - R3.3: With --complement and -f, fields not in the list are output in their original order, separated by the output delimiter.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    items:\n      - R4.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n      - R4.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened, printing the error to stderr. Processing continues for remaining files.\n      - R4.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n      - R4.4: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/cut does not support multi-byte delimiters; -d accepts exactly one byte.\n  - cmd/cut does not implement locale-aware multi-byte character cutting for -c; LC_ALL=C is assumed.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: printf 'a:b:c\\\\n' | cut -d: -f2 produces 'b\\\\n', matching gcut -d: -f2.\"\n  - \"AC2: printf 'abcdef\\\\n' | cut -b2-4 produces 'bcd\\\\n', matching gcut -b2-4.\"\n  - \"AC3: printf 'a:b:c\\\\n' | cut -d: -f1,3 produces 'a:c\\\\n', matching gcut -d: -f1,3.\"\n  - \"AC4: printf 'a:b:c\\\\n' | cut -d: -f2 --complement produces 'a:c\\\\n', matching gcut --complement.\"\n  - \"AC5: printf 'no-delimiter\\\\n' | cut -d: -f2 -s produces empty output, matching gcut -s.\"\n  - \"AC6: Differential test against gcut passes for all required test cases.\"\n  - \"AC7: cmd/cut compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":61,"startLine":1,"totalLines":61}}}
